WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:03.160
- [Narrator] Fertilizer
prices have hit record highs,

00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:06.850
driven by supply shortages
caused by the Ukraine-Russia war,

00:00:06.850 --> 00:00:09.830
along with a host of preexisting factors.

00:00:09.830 --> 00:00:12.610
- It's a challenge for all
of US agriculture right now.

00:00:12.610 --> 00:00:16.820
For farmers who are just walking
in to buy fertilizer today,

00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:18.380
they're twice as extensive

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:20.320
as they would have been last August,

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:23.640
close to three times as
costly as they would have been

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:25.880
in early 2021.

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:27.200
- [Narrator] Fertilizer is typically

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:30.000
among farmers' biggest expenses each year.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.790
And the rising costs are
creating cascading impacts

00:00:32.790 --> 00:00:36.570
for farmer incomes, crop
yields, and food prices.

00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:38.580
- Broadly speaking, that is a big concern

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:40.840
for food production and if
others parts of the world

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:42.340
can't get their fertilizer in,

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:45.340
then it becomes what some CEOs have warned

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:46.980
as a global food crisis.

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:47.920
- [Narrator] So what's driving

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:50.130
these surging fertilizer prices?

00:00:50.130 --> 00:00:53.080
And what does that mean for
farmers and your grocery bills?

00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:57.700
Like many products and
services during the pandemic,

00:00:57.700 --> 00:00:59.810
fertilizer prices have been impacted

00:00:59.810 --> 00:01:02.060
by global supply chain challenges.

00:01:02.060 --> 00:01:02.893
- There's a lot of elements

00:01:02.893 --> 00:01:04.410
that go into manufacturing fertilizer

00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:05.420
and how it's produced.

00:01:05.420 --> 00:01:07.420
It requires different ingredients

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:11.540
and natural gas from all
different parts of the world.

00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:13.570
If one of those things is disrupted,

00:01:13.570 --> 00:01:16.330
it throws off the entire supply chain

00:01:16.330 --> 00:01:18.680
for how fertilizer is produced.

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:20.320
- [Narrator] There are several key factors

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:22.830
behind the global fertilizer supply crunch

00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:24.360
driving high prices.

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:27.720
First, various trade restrictions in 2020

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:31.010
led to a steep drop off
in fertilizer exports.

00:01:31.010 --> 00:01:33.480
In December, the US and European Union

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:35.760
imposed sanctions on Belarus.

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:37.840
This halted exports of potash,

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:40.490
a key ingredient in mineral fertilizers.

00:01:40.490 --> 00:01:44.060
Belarus is the world's
third-largest potash provider.

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:46.350
Meanwhile, trade restrictions from China,

00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:48.640
Turkey, Egypt and Russia led

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:50.420
to reduced fertilizer exports

00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:52.970
in the second half to 2021.

00:01:52.970 --> 00:01:56.340
- Chain banned phosphate exports in 2021.

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:59.780
When a country like China
cuts off its experts,

00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:03.360
that sends prices all over the world up.

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:05.100
- [Narrator] In February, economic fallout

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:06.800
from Russia's invasions of Ukraine

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:09.653
further compounded the strain
on fertilizer production.

00:02:10.550 --> 00:02:13.390
Russia is the world's
largest fertilizer exporter,

00:02:13.390 --> 00:02:15.170
providing around 15%

00:02:15.170 --> 00:02:17.730
of the world's nitrogen-based fertilizers

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:20.950
and 17% of the global potash supply.

00:02:20.950 --> 00:02:23.600
The West's financial
sanctions against Moscow,

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:25.920
combined with transportation
challenges linked

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:29.780
to the war led to a sharp
decline in Russian exports.

00:02:29.780 --> 00:02:32.290
- If you are gonna export out of Russia,

00:02:32.290 --> 00:02:34.830
the insurance on vessels
is very expensive.

00:02:34.830 --> 00:02:37.510
That keeps people from
getting fertilizers out.

00:02:37.510 --> 00:02:40.070
- [Narrator] At the same time,
rising natural gas prices

00:02:40.070 --> 00:02:42.040
have prompted some European factories

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:44.340
to scale back fertilizer production.

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:47.250
Natural gas is another
major Russian export

00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:50.700
and a key ingredient in making
nitrogen-based fertilizer.

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:54.080
Fertilizer is about three
to four times costlier now

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:55.350
than in 2020,

00:02:55.350 --> 00:02:58.340
and farmers around the
world are feeling the pinch.

00:02:58.340 --> 00:03:01.290
- High fertilizer prices
has affected really the flow

00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:03.330
of how our operation does business,

00:03:03.330 --> 00:03:05.000
and really, it comes down to timing

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:07.230
and how much risk does the retailer take?

00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:09.281
How much risk does the farmer take?

00:03:09.281 --> 00:03:10.940
(truck rumbling)

00:03:10.940 --> 00:03:12.900
- [Narrator] Grant Wells
is a third-generation corn

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:15.530
and soybean farmer in Fonda, Iowa.

00:03:15.530 --> 00:03:19.040
He's also the CEO and
owner of Wells AG Supply,

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:21.300
an agriculture retail business.

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:22.730
- When the Russian war hit

00:03:22.730 --> 00:03:26.730
and in February is really when
things started going wild,

00:03:26.730 --> 00:03:28.836
is when things started doubling, tripling

00:03:28.836 --> 00:03:30.580
and so it's really gonna affect

00:03:30.580 --> 00:03:32.240
what the decision making

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:35.490
and what farmers'
profitability's gonna be in 2023.

00:03:35.490 --> 00:03:37.290
- [Narrator] Higher
fertilizer prices translate

00:03:37.290 --> 00:03:39.550
into higher production costs for farmers

00:03:39.550 --> 00:03:42.710
who already operate on
tight profit margins.

00:03:42.710 --> 00:03:45.890
It's also leading to delays
in farmers' planting schedule,

00:03:45.890 --> 00:03:47.710
which could create supply chain snags

00:03:47.710 --> 00:03:50.250
as crops are harvested
later in the season.

00:03:50.250 --> 00:03:53.670
- Most farmers I see delaying
that decision making process

00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:55.200
to later in the season,

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:56.660
instead of maybe November, December,

00:03:56.660 --> 00:03:58.300
they're probably gonna sit on their hands

00:03:58.300 --> 00:03:59.920
and wait 'til Jan, Feb, March,

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:02.330
hoping for some sort of a price reduction,

00:04:02.330 --> 00:04:03.650
and if that doesn't happen,

00:04:03.650 --> 00:04:07.410
then they'll choose to decide
how much can get cut back?

00:04:07.410 --> 00:04:09.530
- [Narrator] Applying less
fertilizer could also lead

00:04:09.530 --> 00:04:11.140
to lower crop yields.

00:04:11.140 --> 00:04:13.270
While some US farmers plant to cut back

00:04:13.270 --> 00:04:15.360
on their overall fertilizer use,

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:16.910
others are shifting their acres

00:04:16.910 --> 00:04:19.370
toward less fertilizer-intensive crops.

00:04:19.370 --> 00:04:20.710
Soybeans, for instance,

00:04:20.710 --> 00:04:23.730
don't require as much
nitrogen-based fertilizer.

00:04:23.730 --> 00:04:25.730
- So it's usually a battle in Iowa

00:04:25.730 --> 00:04:28.110
or in the Midwest
between corn and soybeans

00:04:28.110 --> 00:04:31.130
and so farmers will look
at alternatives to corn.

00:04:31.130 --> 00:04:34.810
Corn is a lot more dominating
on the fertility needs.

00:04:34.810 --> 00:04:36.990
- [Narrator] According to the
US Department of Agriculture,

00:04:36.990 --> 00:04:40.890
the net income for farmers
reached around $119 billion

00:04:40.890 --> 00:04:42.870
in fiscal year 2021,

00:04:42.870 --> 00:04:44.900
the highest in eight years.

00:04:44.900 --> 00:04:48.060
But higher fertilizer prices
and other rising costs,

00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:51.510
such as herbicides are
squeezing their profit margins.

00:04:51.510 --> 00:04:55.390
That means grocery bills
could go up even more in 2022,

00:04:55.390 --> 00:04:57.970
following a year in which
global food prices rose

00:04:57.970 --> 00:04:59.680
to decade highs.

00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:02.040
- It appears that food costs are high

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:04.720
and farmers are benefiting
but that is not the case.

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:06.540
Profit margins are the same,

00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:10.080
consumers are just
unfortunately having to pay more

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:11.910
to put food on their own table.

00:05:11.910 --> 00:05:13.580
- [Narrator] Higher
corn and soybean prices,

00:05:13.580 --> 00:05:15.420
for example, raise the cost

00:05:15.420 --> 00:05:17.610
of animal feed for meat companies,

00:05:17.610 --> 00:05:20.450
which pass along the
increase to consumers.

00:05:20.450 --> 00:05:22.070
- And I think the worst case scenario

00:05:22.070 --> 00:05:25.020
is that we don't have fertilizer at all

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:29.040
to apply to the crops and
then that becomes a feed

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:30.660
and food production issue.

00:05:30.660 --> 00:05:31.980
If we don't produce the crops,

00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:35.250
we can't probably produce
the livestock and the poultry

00:05:35.250 --> 00:05:39.080
and we get into actual
food challenges in the US.

00:05:39.080 --> 00:05:41.160
And that would be a much,
much greater concern

00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:43.030
than what we have today.

00:05:43.030 --> 00:05:44.790
- [Narrator] Meanwhile, some US farmers

00:05:44.790 --> 00:05:48.020
are looking at commercial
fertilizer alternatives.

00:05:48.020 --> 00:05:49.660
Biologicals, for instance,

00:05:49.660 --> 00:05:52.800
are natural fertilizers
containing microorganisms

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:54.530
that enhance soil fertility.

00:05:54.530 --> 00:05:56.530
- The high prices are forcing us

00:05:56.530 --> 00:05:58.630
to look at alternative ways

00:05:58.630 --> 00:06:00.700
to how we've applied fertilizer,

00:06:00.700 --> 00:06:01.780
to be more precise

00:06:01.780 --> 00:06:05.220
and to look at alternatives
like what a biological can do.

00:06:05.220 --> 00:06:06.600
- [Narrator] But switching to biologicals

00:06:06.600 --> 00:06:07.920
or trying new techniques

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:09.680
may carry risks for farmers

00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:12.850
who rely on consistent crop
yields to make a profit.

00:06:12.850 --> 00:06:15.560
- If they try something different one year

00:06:15.560 --> 00:06:17.030
and their yields are down,

00:06:17.030 --> 00:06:18.633
crop doesn't come in like they want,

00:06:18.633 --> 00:06:20.820
then that's it for the year.

00:06:20.820 --> 00:06:22.000
That is their income.

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:22.920
They need it to work.

00:06:22.920 --> 00:06:25.230
They can't really take the risk.

00:06:25.230 --> 00:06:27.670
- [Narrator] As fertilizer
prices continue to rise,

00:06:27.670 --> 00:06:29.490
the agricultural industry braces

00:06:29.490 --> 00:06:32.520
for the long-term impacts
on food productions.

00:06:32.520 --> 00:06:34.490
- The solutions are very difficult to this

00:06:34.490 --> 00:06:38.140
because it comes down to oil production,

00:06:38.140 --> 00:06:39.730
natural gas supplies.

00:06:39.730 --> 00:06:41.400
I think there's a very good chance

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:44.460
that fertilizer costs
could continue to increase

00:06:44.460 --> 00:06:47.570
and we could go into the 2023 crop year

00:06:47.570 --> 00:06:49.240
with even higher fertilizer costs

00:06:49.240 --> 00:06:50.660
than what we've had in 2022.

00:06:51.523 --> 00:06:54.273
(dramatic music)

